WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
A secure REST API is built from repeatable decisions.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:17.160
Define the route contract, validate untrusted input, authenticate the caller, authorize the action and object, shape the response, log responsibly, and test both accepted and rejected behavior.

3
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.940
Each step reduces ambiguity before the API reaches production.

4
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.580
TypeScript helps when it describes safe states clearly.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:33.720
It works best with strict compiler settings, runtime schemas, safe narrowing, and response models that separate internal data from public output.

6
00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.780
Types should make reviewed behavior easier to see, not hide uncertainty behind broad assertions.

7
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:46.340
Authentication, authorization, output control, middleware, telemetry, and delivery governance are connected.

8
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.400
A token check without object policy is incomplete.

9
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.140
Validation without safe output design still leaves exposure risk.

10
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.920
Logging without sensitivity rules can create new problems.

11
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.740
Testing without production monitoring leaves teams blind to drift.

12
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:18.460
The practical roadmap is straightforward: know the boundary, make the contract explicit, enforce the contract at runtime, check identity and permission on the server, return only what is needed, set defensive operational limits, and keep the pattern alive through review and CI.

13
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.960
Durable API security is a lifecycle habit, not a single feature.

14
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:27.960
When this roadmap is applied consistently, teams spend less time guessing and more time improving.

15
00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.380
New routes can follow known patterns.

16
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.480
Old routes can be audited against the same expectations.

17
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.780
Production signals can be connected back to design decisions, tests, and ownership.
